# ssh hosts on the home network. Deployed by home-manager as
# ~/.ssh/config.homelab, and pulled in by ~/.ssh/config with
# `Include ~/.ssh/config.homelab`, ahead of its `Host *` block. ssh_config is
# FIRST-MATCH-WINS, so that ordering is what lets these entries answer before
# the catch-all defaults do.
#
# IT LIVES HERE, NOT IN THE HOMELAB REPO. It was briefly a symlink into
# homelab/homelab-setup/ssh/, on the reasoning that the guest inventory belongs
# beside the thing that creates the guests. That reasoning is sound and was
# still dropped, because it bought one dependency that ssh cannot complain
# about: the Include pointed at a path in another repo, and moving, renaming or
# re-checking-out that repo would dangle it. ssh SKIPS a dangling Include
# silently -- exit 0, nothing on stderr -- so the failure mode was "the homelab
# aliases quietly stop resolving" while git and everything else kept working,
# with nothing pointing at the cause. One repo owning every file it Includes
# costs a little duplication of intent and removes that entire class of failure.
#
# NOTE: this repo is PUBLIC, so these addresses are public too. That is a
# deliberate exception to ~/.ssh/config's own policy that network descriptions
# stay untracked, and it is narrow: RFC1918 only, unreachable from the internet,
# and this subnet was ALREADY published by the homelab repo through
# infrastructure/configs/metallb/ipaddresspool.yaml and its terraform provider.
# Nothing routable and nothing naming an organisation goes in this file --
# those belong in ~/.ssh/config.local, which is untracked and stays that way.
#
# KEEP IN SYNC WITH REALITY. Creating a guest adds an entry here; destroying one
# removes it (and needs `ssh-keygen -R <ip>` locally, which is machine state and
# cannot live in git). The proxmox skills in the homelab repo drive that -- see
# its .claude/skills/proxmox-{vm,ct}/SKILL.md, which point at this path.
#
# THOSE SKILLS COMMIT INTO THIS REPO, which they do not own, so they scope BOTH
# git verbs and both are load-bearing:
#
#   git -C ~/.dotfiles add dotfiles/ssh/config.homelab
#   git -C ~/.dotfiles commit -m "..." -- dotfiles/ssh/config.homelab
#
#   the `add`         because a bare `git commit` commits THE WHOLE INDEX, and
#                     this repo has held a stale staged copy of an ssh file
#                     where that would have published the stale version and
#                     silently dropped the working-tree edit.
#   the `-- <path>`   because a bare commit would still sweep up whatever else
#                     happened to be staged here, under an unrelated message.
#
# Neither replaces the other. `commit -- <path>` ignores the rest of the index
# but the pathspec must still resolve against a file git already knows -- on an
# untracked one it fails with "did not match any file(s) known to git", tested.
# It works here only because this file is tracked. Do not conclude the `add` is
# optional and drop it: the next NEW file breaks, and the tempting fix is to
# drop the scoping instead, which brings back the sweep.
#
# ABSENT ON PURPOSE, NOT LOST: four kubernetes VMs -- dev-master, dev-worker,
# cks-master, cks-worker, 192.168.1.203-.206. They were removed before this file
# was ever tracked anywhere, so no repo's history shows them leaving.
# Verified at the time: all four fail to ping, their IdentityFile
# ~/.ssh/remote_vm does not exist on this machine, and `qm list` / `pct list` on
# the node show only template 9000 and CT 100. They are entries for guests that
# exist nowhere reachable -- presumably they lived on the second cluster node,
# which is powered off. Restoring them would give four Host blocks pointing at
# nothing, with a missing key. This file is an inventory meant to match reality,
# so they stay out rather than being commented back in: a commented block is a
# claim about intent, and intent belongs in docs, not an ssh config.

# --- hypervisor --------------------------------------------------------------
# Proxmox VE node. `pve` is a synonym because the node calls itself that and the
# docs use both. See .claude/skills/proxmox-{vm,ct}/SKILL.md.
Host proxmox pve
  HostName 192.168.1.110
  User root
  Port 22
  IdentityFile ~/.ssh/proxmox

# --- guests ------------------------------------------------------------------
# CT 100 -- Pi-hole, network DNS + ad blocking. Static IP because it cannot get
# its own address from the DHCP server it is meant to eventually replace.
# See homelab-setup/pihole/.
Host pihole
  HostName 192.168.1.111
  User root
  Port 22
  IdentityFile ~/.ssh/proxmox
